Large CSV files

Reading in chunks
DataFrame::fromCsvChunked() reads a file a chunk of rows at a time, as readr’s read_delim_chunked() does. It passes each chunk to a callback with the 1-based index of the chunk’s first row, and stops if the callback returns false. DataFrame::csvChunks() returns the same chunks as a generator keyed by that index. Memory depends on the chunk size, so a file larger than the memory limit can be filtered down as it is read. Here the 336,776 nycflights13 departures from New York City in 2013 are filtered to those bound for Los Angeles, 50,000 rows at a time:
$chunks = DataFrame::csvChunks(
    'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/phpstats/datasets/'
        .'main/csv/flights.csv',
    chunkSize: 50_000,
);

$kept = [];
foreach ($chunks as $chunk) {
    $kept[] = $chunk-&gt;filter(col('dest')-&gt;eq('LAX'));
}
$lax = DataFrame::concat($kept);

echo $lax-&gt;groupBy(['origin'])-&gt;summarize(['flights' =&gt; n()]);
# A tibble: 2 × 2
  origin flights
  &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
1 EWR       4912
2 JFK      11262
Only Newark and JFK have flights to Los Angeles: 4,912 and 11,262.
Types not given are guessed from the first chunk, and every later chunk is parsed as those types. As in readr, guessMax defaults to the chunk size. Give every type, such as types: ['.default' =&gt; 'c'], to skip guessing. A value in a later chunk that does not fit its column’s type becomes NA. Factors without levels take their levels from each chunk.
Every other option works as it does in fromCsv(). Each chunk’s problems raise their own warning, with rows numbered from the start of the file.
With the phit extension loaded, chunks are read natively with Phit\CsvReader, with the same results as the PHP reader.


Memory
A whole-file read holds every value as an element of a PHP array, with either reader, so its memory grows with the file. fromCsvChunked() holds one chunk at a time, so its memory follows the chunk size. An aggregate that can be combined, such as a count or a sum, can be computed for each chunk and the results added up. This script counts the penguins of each species, 100 rows at a time:
$totals = [];
$count = function (DataFrame $chunk) use (&$totals) {
    $counts = $chunk
        -&gt;groupBy(['species'])
        -&gt;summarize(['n' =&gt; n()]);
    foreach ($counts as $row) {
        $totals[$row-&gt;species] ??= 0;
        $totals[$row-&gt;species] += $row-&gt;n;
    }
};

DataFrame::fromCsvChunked(
    'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/phpstats/datasets/'
        .'main/csv/penguins.csv', $count, chunkSize: 100);
ksort($totals);

echo tibble(
    species: array_keys($totals),
    n: array_values($totals),
);
# A tibble: 3 × 2
  species       n
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
