For a file too large to hold as PHP arrays, keep the data in Polars, and only the result comes back to PHP. PolarsTable keeps phibble’s and phplyr’s API, as the Backends page describes. phpolars’ lazy CSV example queries a large file with Polars’ own API.

Writing and reading JSON
toJson() writes a list of records, and toJson('columns') writes an object of columns. NA is written as null and kept in every record. DataFrame::fromJson() reads either shape and detects which one it has.
$df = tibble(species: ['Adelie', 'Adelie'], body_mass_g: [3750, null]);
echo $df-&gt;toJson(), "\n";
echo $df-&gt;toJson('columns'), "\n";
echo DataFrame::fromJson('{"faa": ["EWR", "JFK"], "alt": [18, 13]}');
[{"species":"Adelie","body_mass_g":3750},{"species":"Adelie","body_mass_g":null}]
{"species":["Adelie","Adelie"],"body_mass_g":[3750,null]}
# A tibble: 2 × 2
  faa     alt
  &lt;chr&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 EWR      18
2 JFK      13

Laravel collections
DataFrame::fromCollection() reads any object with an all() or toArray() method, such as a Laravel Collection. The items may be arrays, objects, or Eloquent models. toCollection() returns an Illuminate\Support\Collection of row arrays and throws when Laravel is not installed.
$collection = new class
{
    public function all(): array
    {
        return [
            ['species' =&gt; 'Adelie', 'body_mass_g' =&gt; 3750],
            (object) ['species' =&gt; 'Gentoo', 'body_mass_g' =&gt; 4500],
        ];
    }
};

echo DataFrame::fromCollection($collection);
# A tibble: 2 × 2
  species body_mass_g
  &lt;chr&gt;         &lt;int&gt;
1 Adelie         3750
2 Gentoo         4500

Joins
join() between two tables on the same PDO connection compiles to a SQL join. The output columns follow DataFrame::join(): x’s columns, then y’s other columns, with .x and .y suffixes on clashing names. NA keys match each other, as in dplyr, through IS in SQLite, IS NOT DISTINCT FROM in PostgreSQL, and &lt;=&gt; in MySQL. Pass naMatches: false for SQL’s usual equality. This example joins each airport’s mean 2013 temperature, from nycflights13’s weather, to its name and altitude from the airports table:
$weather = tbl($pdo, 'weather');
$airports = tbl($pdo, 'airports');

$joined = $weather
    -&gt;groupBy(['origin'])
    -&gt;summarize(['temp' =&gt; mean(col('temp'), na_rm: true)])
    -&gt;join($airports, [['origin', 'faa']], 'left');

echo $joined-&gt;showQuery(), "\n\n";
echo $joined-&gt;arrange(['origin'])-&gt;collect();


The SQL from showQuery()

SELECT "origin", "temp", "name", "alt" FROM (SELECT jq3x."origin" AS "origin", jq3x."temp" AS "temp", jq3y."name" AS "name", jq3y."alt" AS "alt", jq3x."__phibble_row" AS "__phibble_row", jq3y."__phibble_row" AS "__phibble_row_1" FROM (SELECT "origin", "temp", ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ("origin" IS NULL), "origin") AS "__phibble_row" FROM (SELECT "origin" AS "origin", (CASE WHEN COUNT("temp") = 0 THEN NULL ELSE AVG(CAST("temp" AS REAL)) END) AS "temp" FROM (SELECT *, rowid AS "__phibble_row" FROM "weather") AS q1 GROUP BY "origin") AS q2) AS jq3x LEFT JOIN (SELECT *, rowid AS "__phibble_row" FROM "airports") AS jq3y ON (jq3x."origin" IS jq3y."faa")) AS q4 ORDER BY ("__phibble_row" IS NULL), "__phibble_row", ("__phibble_row_1" IS NULL), "__phibble_row_1"

